# Cloudflare Pages.
#
# Only /api/* starts a Function (see _routes.json). A file that exists is served as that file, so
# /assets/* goes straight from the CDN: no Function call, no CPU budget, no request against the
# daily limit. It used to be a catch-all Function just to stop a missing script from becoming the
# app page, and that cost one Function invocation per script, stylesheet and chunk on every visit.
#
# The app's addresses are listed one by one rather than with `/*` for the same reason: a
# catch-all also catches a missing /assets/<hash>.js, answers with index.html and status 200, and
# /assets is cached for a year - the browser then keeps a page where a script should be and every
# refresh is blank. An address that is not listed here is an honest 404.
#
# A new screen needs a line here as well as a case in src/lib/router.ts;
# scripts/deploy-files.test.mjs fails when the two disagree.
#
# Rewrite to `/`, never to `/index.html`. Pages turns /index.html into / (pretty URLs), sees a loop
# and silently IGNORES such a rule. Until 404.html existed that went unnoticed: with no top-level
# 404.html, Pages answers every miss with the app page, so the screens opened anyway, and so did a
# missing /assets/<hash>.js (status 200, cached for a year). public/404.html ends that fallback,
# so these rules must really work. `/` itself needs no rule: it is index.html.
